AB  - Denoising diffusion models have emerged as the dominant paradigm in deep generative modeling, surpassing Generative Adversarial Networks, Variational Autoencoders, and Normalizing Flows in sample quality, mode coverage, and training stability. Despite rapid progress, existing surveys lack formal mathematical unification, quantitative cross-paradigm benchmarks, and coverage of discrete diffusion language models or agricultural imaging. This survey addresses these gaps through five contributions. First, we establish a formal unification of Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models, Score-Based Generative Models, and the Stochastic Differential Equation framework, deriving an explicit algebraic equivalence between noise prediction and score estimation and explaining accelerated samplers including DDIM, DPM-Solver, and Consistency Models as discretizations of the unified probability flow ODE. Second, we provide the first quantitative cross-paradigm comparison using concrete benchmark values for sample quality, mode coverage, negative log-likelihood, and inference cost. Third, we present dedicated mathematical treatment of Flow Matching, Rectified Flow, Classifier-Free Guidance, and ControlNet, alongside comprehensive coverage of SDXL, Stable Diffusion 3, Flux, Sora, and Open-Sora. Fourth, we provide the first survey-level formal treatment of discrete diffusion language models including D3PM and Masked Diffusion Language Model with quantitative language modeling benchmarks. Fifth, we introduce a formal characterization of the biological fidelity gap in agricultural imaging, a critical limitation across all scientific imaging applications. Seventeen open research challenges spanning theoretical foundations, computational efficiency, discrete data modeling, and alignment are systematically identified. As a literature review, this work does not report original experimental results; curated references to publicly available implementations of core frameworks are provided in Appendix to support reproducibility.
