AB  - Collaborative in-network processing in the Internet of Things (IoT) via the diffusion least mean-square (DLMS) algorithm faces a critical trilemma: it exposes sensitive node-specific data, incurs substantial communication overhead, and its privacy protection via differential privacy (DP) often leads to significant performance loss. This article introduces a compressive DP DLMS (C-DP-DLMS) algorithm that breaks this deadlock. Our core innovation lies in a randomized compression operator that serves a dual purpose: it drastically reduces communication load while actively mitigating the performance degradation induced by DP noise. The algorithm guarantees rigorous (is an element of, delta) -DP by injecting calibrated noise into local updates prior to the compressive diffusion process. Theoretical analysis and extensive simulations on IoT target localization demonstrate that our algorithm achieves a marked improvement in estimation accuracy alongside a substantial reduction in communication load compared to existing privacy-preserving DLMS alternatives, thereby effectively navigating the privacy-accuracy-communication trilemma and establishing a practical and scalable solution for secure distributed sensing.
