AB  - This paper studies censored regression over route-constrained incremental adaptive networks. Each node observes a censored version of an underlying latent linear regression model, and the objective is to estimate a common parameter vector from streaming censored data. Unlike diffusion-based strategies that rely on neighborhood combination, the proposed framework propagates a single estimate sequentially along a prescribed incremental route. To compensate for the sample-selection bias induced by censoring, each node performs a local adaptive bias-correction step based on a probit model and the inverse Mills ratio. The resulting bias-compensated innovation is then used in an LMS-type incremental update of the circulating estimate. A mean and mean-square performance analysis is developed using weight-error recursions and variance relations, leading to mean-stability conditions and a steady-state network MSD expression in terms of effective censored-regression statistics. Simulation results show that ordinary incremental LMS suffers from a bias-induced error floor, whereas the proposed incremental censored regression algorithm achieves lower network MSD than non-cooperative censored regression and diffusion censored regression (d-CR) by exploiting sequential cooperation. The theoretical steady-state MSD is also shown to agree closely with simulation results under the ideal bias-corrected setting.
