AB  - Compared with the normalized least mean square (NLMS) algorithm, the decorrelation-based NLMS algorithm accelerates the convergence rate for colored inputs but degrades the steady-state performance. To overcome this, we propose a partial-decorrelation NLMS (PD-NLMS) algorithm, in which a proportion control factor is introduced to balance the original and decorrelated inputs. First, we provide a detailed theoretical analysis of the mean and mean-square behavior of the PD-NLMS algorithm. Theoretical results are derived for its stability condition and steady-state expressions, highlighting the roles of the proportion control factor and the step-size in determining PD-NLMS algorithm's performance. Then, to enable the PD-NLMS algorithm to achieve fast convergence rate while maintaining the steady-state error comparable to that of the NLMS algorithm, we develop a variable proportion control factor scheme. Based on this scheme, two improved algorithms are proposed: the partialadaptive-decorrelation NLMS with variable proportion control factor (VPAD-NLMS) algorithm, which adopts the adaptive decorrelation scheme, and the partial-fast-decorrelation NLMS with variable proportion control factor (VPFD-NLMS) algorithm, which employs the fast decorrelation scheme. Simulations validate the theoretical analysis and demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed algorithms.
