AB  - Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable generative and reasoning capabilities, yet their application to structured financial data remains limited by schema-agnostic representations and task specialization. Existing synthetic data generators often fail to preserve multivariate dependencies across transaction attributes, while conventional fraud detection models rely on static feature engineering and lack adaptability to evolving fraud patterns. To address these limitations, we propose UniFi-LLM, a Unified Large Language Model for Synthetic Financial Data Generation and Fraud Prediction. UniFi-LLM leverages schema-aware prompt templates, column-conditioned LoRA adapters, and structured output enforcement to learn both generative and discriminative objectives within a single model. The framework synthesizes high-fidelity financial records that preserve statistical realism and relational consistency, while simultaneously enabling fraud risk prediction conditioned on transaction features. Comprehensive experiments on open-source credit card datasets demonstrate that UniFi-LLM outperforms traditional GAN, VAE, and diffusion-based generators in fidelity metrics and achieves superior AUCROC and F1 scores for fraud classification. By unifying generation and prediction under a single LLM backbone, UniFi-LLM significantly reduces retraining overhead.
