AB  - The grain-boundary diffusion process (GBDP) is the leading route to high coercivity in NdFeB permanent magnets with reduced heavy-rare-earth (HRE) content, and its rapidly growing literature is increasingly difficult to synthesize. We built a retrieval-augmented-generation (RAG) system over a corpus of 311 GBDP papers that uses lightweight, CPU-only sentence embeddings, section-aware chunking, and source-cited answer generation, and used it to compare three large language models: the commercial GPT-4o and Gemini 3.5 Flash, and the locally served open-weight Llama 3.2. To separate the language model from the retriever, we adopted a controlled design in which retrieval was held constant so that all three models received an identical evidence set for each question, and answers were assessed qualitatively for faithfulness to the retrieved passages and for citation correctness. Across three representative questions (the advantage of GBDP, its diffusion sources and the role of HRE), the models' factual grounding was comparable once the retrieved context was informative; removing duplicate documents from the index improved the retrieval of answer-bearing passages and reduced unsupported additions in the evaluated cases. The principal difference was citation reliability: both commercial models attributed every claim correctly, whereas the local model extracted correct content but omitted or misapplied source citations. Faithfulness thus emerged as a property of the retrieval stage as much as of the model. We conclude that RAG is a practical aid for consolidating dispersed GBDP knowledge, and that a local open-weight model is a viable privacy-preserving option provided its attribution is verified.
