AB  - Recent advances in AI-powered archaeological discoveries have garnered widespread attention, ranging from language model-enhanced inscription restoration to vision-based fragment reassembly. Despite these commendable pioneering efforts, a significant gap remains between academic findings and their direct application in archaeological research practice, particularly in the context of Dunhuang manuscript studies. To bridge this gap, we propose DHelper, the first collaborative toolkit designed specifically for Dunhuang philologists, consisting of distinct tools aimed at addressing downstream research tasks. Our toolkit introduces a new paradigm that integrates large language models with domain-specific models to facilitate interdisciplinary research. By streamlining processes such as manuscript preprocessing, fragment concatenation, knowledge discovery, and restoration, DHelper not only enhances research efficiency but also advocates for increased attention to the application of cutting-edge techniques in uncovering new insights from ancient texts. Ultimately, this work aims to contribute to the flourishing field of Dunhuang studies and promote further exploration of the rich cultural heritage encapsulated within these manuscripts.
