AB  - Influence Maximization (IM) aims to identify key users (seeds) to maximize information diffusion in social net works. However, existing approaches seldom explore how content semantics interact with user preferences to guide personalized diffusion. This work defines the Content-Guided Influence Maximization (CGIM) problem, which unifies structural influence estimation and semantic relevance optimization within a single framework. To address CGIM, we propose MIA, a retrieval-augmented influence analysis framework that integrates multi-modal structural modeling, influence estimation, and retrieval-augmented generation using large language models (LLMs). Specifically, MIA constructs a multi-modal knowledge graph to capture user-content relations, estimates content-aware susceptibility via contrastive learning, and optimizes propagation through a multi-layer influ ence maximization algorithm before executing retrieval-augmented content generation. Experiments on four datasets show that MIA effectively balances diffusion efficiency and semantic alignment, providing a unified and interpretable solution for CGIM in heterogeneous social networks.
