AB  - The synergistic fusion of multimodal remote sensing data can substantially enhance classification performance, yet most existing approaches remain dominated by visual information and overlook the high-level semantic cues provided by language. Although vision-language models (VLMs) such as the contrastive language-image pretraining (CLIP) framework show promise, their effectiveness in remote sensing is limited by significant visual domain gaps and insufficient semantic descriptions, as generic prompts fail to capture the complex attributes of land-cover classes. To address this issue, we propose multimodal attribute-anchored prompts CLIP (MAPCLIP), a new framework for few-shot multimodal remote sensing classification. Specifically, unsupervised masked diffusion pretraining first mitigates domain gaps. Meanwhile, the multimodal attribute-anchored prompt (MAP) strategy leverages LLMs to generate dynamic descriptions, enriching textual semantics. Finally, a prototype-guided image-enhanced text refinement (Proto-IET) module synergizes them, using pretrained visual prototypes for robust alignment. Extensive experiments on multiple multimodal remote sensing datasets under various few-shot settings demonstrate that MAPCLIP achieves consistently strong performance and exhibits broad cross-modality generalization beyond specific modality pairs.
