AB  - This paper investigates the diffusion of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) across sociodemographic groups in Europe. To this aim, we construct occupation-level indicators of GenAI exposure using task-level evaluations from large language models. Specifically, each task is scored according to the probability of being complemented or replaced by GenAI tools, and these assessments are then aggregated to generate occupation-specific exposure measures. We merge these indicators with microdata from the 2024 European Labour Force Survey and estimate logistic regression models to examine differences in the probability of employment in occupations highly exposed to GenAI complementarity and replacement across European regions and sociodemographic subgroups. We document substantial geographic heterogeneity: Northern and Western Europe exhibit the highest shares of workers in highly exposed occupations, whereas Eastern and parts of Southern Europe display lower exposure levels. Workers born in non-European low- and middle-income countries and individuals reporting health limitations are less likely to be employed in occupations with high GenAI complementarity risk. Workers with two or more children under age five are less exposed to GenAI replacement risk. Overall, the results reveal pronounced regional and sociodemographic divides in occupational exposure to GenAI across Europe.
