KW  - Diffusion
KW  - Large Language Model (LLM)
KW  - Network intrusion detection system (NIDS)
AB  - Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) are critical components of modern cybersecurity infrastructure. Developing robust machine learning (ML)-driven NIDS requires extensive network traffic datasets, where flow-based analysis is essential for detecting suspicious traffic patterns and improving model performance. Since network traffic data often contains sensitive information and is subject to strict privacy and security regulations, there is a notable scarcity of publicly accessible, realistic network traffic flow datasets for research. Moreover, many existing open-source datasets are highly class imbalanced, posing significant challenges for researchers. While some researchers have created NIDS datasets using testbeds or network simulations, configuring complex network topologies is often time-consuming, costly, and difficult to customize. To the best of our knowledge, the generation of realistic network flow datasets remains a major obstacle in NIDS research. Recently, generative artificial intelligence (AI) methods, including large language models (LLMs), have demonstrated strong capabilities in generating various types of synthetic data across domains. However, to date, no existing work has explored their application in generating datasets to facilitate NIDS research. This study establishes a comprehensive evaluation pipeline to assess the effectiveness of several representative and state-of-the-art generative AI models for synthetic flow data generation in the NIDS domain. Our extensive experiments on three widely used NIDS datasets demonstrate that ML models trained on synthetic flow data generated by LLM-based generative AI methods can achieve macro-average F1 scores of up to 86%, outperforming other generative AI approaches in classifying benign and attack types.
