TI  - Conflict-free scheduling of twin automated stacking cranes considering container reshuffling using a discrete flower pollination algorithm
T2  - ADVANCED ENGINEERING INFORMATICS
LA  - English
KW  - Twin automatic stacking cranes
KW  - Automated container terminal
KW  - Flower pollination algorithm
KW  - Scheduling
KW  - YARD CRANES
KW  - OPTIMIZATION
KW  - CONSUMPTION
KW  - DISPATCH
KW  - MODEL
AB  - Automated container Terminals (ACTs) have been increasingly appearing in maritime transport. One essential piece of automated equipment for handling containers in ACTs is the Twin Automated Stacking Cranes (TASC), which must maintain a safe distance to avoid collisions when operating. However, TASCs are likely bottlenecks in an ACT due to the configuration with only two cranes. Improving the operational efficiency of TASCs has become a critical issue, prompting this research to focus on the TASC scheduling (TASCS) problem. To address the TASCS problem, this research first formulates it as a mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) model with an objective and relevant constraints. Specifically, the objective aims to minimize the total operational time of container tasks and the waiting time of TASC, while considering safety distance and operational sequence constraints. To address the computational time intractability, a discrete flower pollination algorithm (DFPA) combined with an overlap resolution procedure is proposed to handle large instances. The DFPA improves the standard FPA, enabling it to handle discrete problems, perform adaptive movement for pollens, and increase solution diversity. The DFPA focuses on generating alternative container task sequences, while the overlap resolution procedure ensures the feasibility of the solutions. Experimental results show that the DFPA outperforms the standard FPA, genetic algorithm (GA), and the first-come-first-served (FCFS) heuristic, as well as metaheuristics including ant colony optimization (ACO), firefly algorithm (FA), and particle swarm optimization (PSO). Extensive experiments show that the DFPA with a handshake area setting in the middle part of the block is not advantageous.
