AB  - In the digital economy, vocational art design education suffers from inadequate creativity, weak industry linkage, and simplistic evaluation. This study develops a dual-modal generative evaluation engine using vision-language models and low-rank adaptation (LoRA) lightweight fine-tuning. With a curriculum-competency-post knowledge graph embedded with Myers-Briggs type indicator (MBTI) traits and market adaptation indicators, it achieves dynamic curriculum arrangement and accurate coach-student matching. Integrated with Few-Shot Diffusion generation, multi-agent grouping, and extended reality (XR) adaptive rendering, an immersive simulation training framework is constructed. Practical verification proves this model effectively enhances students' design competence and industry adaptability, providing a replicable paradigm for school-enterprise cooperation. Key implementation norms are summarized to support the development of immersive simulation-based art design education.
