LA  - English
KW  - Seismic processing foundation model
KW  - Construction of seismic training dataset
KW  - Diffusion model
KW  - Mamba
KW  - TRACE INTERPOLATION
AB  - Foundation models have achieved remarkable success in natural language processing and computer vision by large-scale pretraining and self-supervised learning, and are increasingly being explored in geophysics. However, their development paradigm cannot be directly transferred to seismic data processing because of limited publicly available data, significant domain discrepancies across survey areas, and constrained computational resources. This paper reviews the evolution of foundation models, focusing on the development of large language models, large vision models, and vision-language models, together with their applications in exploration geophysics, seismology, and related geophysical disciplines. Focusing on ill-posed inverse problems in seismic data processing, we summarize the major challenges in pretraining dataset construction, network architecture design, self-supervised learning, and cross-task transfer, and outline future research directions. As a representative case study, we present a lightweight seismic processing foundation model (SPFM) to illustrate a development pipeline for geophysical foundation models under limited data and computational resources. The pipeline comprises three stages: pretraining dataset construction, foundation model development, and downstream task adaptation. A representative pretraining dataset is built by integrating publicly available seismic data with diffusion-model-based augmentation and optimized by dimensionality reduction, clustering, and stratified sampling. A lightweight self-supervised framework based on the selective structured state space model (Mamba) is then adopted to efficiently learn seismic representations, and the pretrained model is transferred to multiple processing tasks by a unified fine-tuning strategy. Experimental results demonstrate that SPFM achieves strong cross-task generalization and competitive performance while maintaining low computational cost, providing an efficient solution for developing foundation models in geophysics.As per instructions Article highlights are mandatory for this journal. Kindly check and confirm.Thanks for your valuable comments. Following the journal requirements, we have prepared the Article Highlights based on the main contributions of this paper. The highlights are provided as follows: 1. By integrating a representative and non-redundant pre-training dataset with a Mamba-based network architecture and self-supervised pre-training strategy, a lightweight seismic processing foundation model (SPFM) is proposed to achieve efficient seismic representation learning under limited data and computational resources. 2. A pre-training dataset construction strategy combining diffusion-model-based augmentation, feature clustering, and stratified sampling is proposed to ensure sample representativeness, effectively reduce data redundancy, and enhance the completeness of the pre-training dataset. 3. The proposed foundation model enables effective transfer across multiple seismic processing tasks, including denoising, interpolation, low-frequency extrapolation, and resolution enhancement, demonstrating strong generalization capability and computational efficiency while achieving comparable or superior performance to existing state-of-the-art methods.
