AB  - Modern Deep Neural Network (DNN) exhibits a pattern where multiple sub-models are executed, guided by control flows such as loops and switch/merge operations. This dynamic nature introduces complexities in batching the requests of such DNNs for efficient execution on GPUs. In this paper, we present NeuStream, a programming model and runtime system for serving deep learning workloads using stream processing. NeuStream decomposes the inference workflow into modules and forms them into a streaming processing system where a request flows through. Based on such abstraction, NeuStream is able to batch requests at fine-grained module granularity. To maximize serving goodput, NeuStream exploits a two-level scheduling approach to decide the best batching requests and resource allocation for each module while satisfying service level objectives (SLOs). Our evaluation of NeuStream on a set of modern DNNs like Large Language Models (LLM) and diffusion models, etc., shows that NeuStream significantly improves goodput compared to state-of-the-art DNN serving systems.
