AB  - Technological innovation bears many similarities to the emergence of new species, and this has motivated social and management scientists to make evolutionary analogies of technological change to natural evolution. Like natural evolution, innovation can be conceived as a three-stage process, though obviously the mechanisms remain very different. Innovators purposively generate variation through recombining extant technologies, rather than genetic mixing. Along with those that control development resources, innovators select some variants for further development. Finally, the larger community retains successful variants in the artifactual gene pool, and these provide the basis for the next cycle of variants. We organize the literature into these three stages, speculate on how generative artificial intelligence and large language models and the availability of massively increased data will change the evolutionary process of innovation, and close by proposing that innovations in Bayesian hierarchical models might be particularly suited to the study of evolutionary models of innovation.
