AB  - Artificial intelligence is rapidly evolving from a passive tool into an active participant in collective decision-making. However, existing theoretical studies primarily focus on high-level mechanism design and assume perfect preference representation by AI, overlooking the strategic challenge of information revelation. We address this gap by developing, to the best of our knowledge, the first game-theoretic framework that explicitly links representation fidelity to the amount of information revealed in collective settings. Our equilibrium analysis reveals a striking strategic dynamic: Members with conflicting preferences reveal more information than those with aligned preferences, because the former actively compete to steer the outcome while the latter tend to free-ride on others' revelations. Larger groups may suffer greater decision distortion due to diffusion of responsibility. Since no member achieves perfect representation at equilibrium, we benchmark digital representatives against direct human participation. We show that digital representatives can closely approximate manual decisions when preferences conflict. Numerical results further show that digital representation can outperform direct participation when communication costs are high or AI capability is advanced, as it sharply reduces the combined communication cost and preference loss. These findings provide practical guidance for deploying digital representatives in real-world collective decision-making.
