TI  - CountLEG: Compositional referring expression counting framework via Latent Expression Guidance
T2  - PATTERN RECOGNITION
LA  - English
KW  - Referring expression counting
KW  - Vision-language models
KW  - Text-guided object counting
AB  - Referring Expression Counting (REC) requires a model to simultaneously understand fine-grained linguistic attributes and localize multiple object instances, which remains challenging for existing methods. Current REC baselines suffer from semantic fossilization: they treat referring expressions as monolithic templates, failing to generalize attribute semantics to unseen combinations and struggling to distinguish minimal lexical variations. To address this, we propose CountLEG, a novel framework centered on Latent Expression Guidance (LEG). Our key insight is to explicitly perturb the textual embedding space via stochastic dropout, generating semantically diversified latent expressions that preserve the core object category while exploring attribute variations. Specifically, a Latent Expression Generation Module (LEGM) synthesizes these variants using dropout and an MLP, and a Latent Semantic Supplementation (LSS) mechanism fuses them with the original expression through dual-path cross-attention in a diffusion-based U-Net. This design enhances the model's sensitivity to fine-grained semantics and reduces its reliance on spurious correlations. Extensive experiments on the REC-8 K benchmark demonstrate that CountLEG achieves competitive results compared to baselines, and significantly outperforms prior approaches (e.g., CAD-GD) in addressing the semantic fossilization problem, highlighting the effectiveness of latent expression guidance for fine-grained attribute understanding. Code is available at https://github.com/psbetter/CountLEG.
