AU  - McConville, T
TI  - Materials discovery is a composition problem: the case for agentic AI over bigger models
T2  - DIGITAL DISCOVERY
LA  - English
AB  - For over a decade, the materials artificial intelligence (AI) community has chased ever-larger individual specialist models: graph neural networks for property prediction, universal interatomic potentials for atomistic simulation, and diffusion models for generative design. These efforts have produced high-quality results at each pillar of an autonomous discovery system. Yet machine learning has not visibly accelerated the rate of materials discovery at the level these advances would predict. We argue that this gap reflects a misallocation of effort. The remaining bottleneck is not the predictive accuracy of any individual model; it is the absence of a layer that composes specialist models, in context, into the multi-step scientific investigations that real discovery requires. Large language model agents, equipped with standardised tool-use protocols and a fast-growing ecosystem of materials-specific tools, are now mature enough to be that composition layer themselves. We diagnose the composition gap between the functional pillars of the autonomous materials discovery and optimization (AMDO) frame, show through recent existence proofs that agentic AI now fills it, and propose four concrete shifts the materials AI community should prioritise: standardised agent-accessible interfaces for scientific codes, benchmarks for agent execution quality, provenance and verifiability as defaults, and composition-aware specialist models. Reorienting effort toward the composition layer is the highest-leverage step available to the field today.
