AB  - Predicting participants in tag-based information cascades remains a challenging task in social network analysis. Although graph neural networks (GNNs) have achieved strong performance in cascade prediction, existing methods still suffer from two major limitations: they often rely heavily on explicit follower-followee relationships while overlooking latent information channels between disconnected users, and they inadequately handle topic dispersion in hashtag-driven cascades, leading to suboptimal performance and limited interpretability. To address these limitations, we propose an Interest-aware Heterogeneous Diffusion (IHD) model for tag-based cascade prediction. IHD first restructures cascades into interest-aware heterogeneous diffusion graphs (IHDGs) through semantic alignment, enabling the modeling of latent propagation pathways among users who are not directly connected in the social graph. It then employs a temporal graph neural architecture over evolving IHDGs to learn context-sensitive user representations that capture both structural propagation patterns and interest-driven engagement behaviors. Finally, IHD aggregates user representations across multiple hashtag-associated IHDGs to characterize users' cross-interest susceptibility. Experimental results on Weibo and Twitter datasets show that IHD consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in both Hits@K and MAP@K metrics, with relative improvements of up to 29% on the Weibo dataset. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of incorporating semantic interest signals into cascade prediction.
