AB  - This paper introduces a Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (MRAG) framework that autonomously reconstructs antenna geometries from scientific literature. Most of the scientific knowledge about antenna design is stored in unstructured documents, where important geometric information is spread out across figures, captions, and descriptive text. The proposed pipeline takes figures and contextual text from PDF files, uses vision-language models to make unified multimodal embeddings, and then stores them in a vector database for semantic retrieval. A retrieval-augmented reasoning pipeline processes retrieved multimodal evidence to find antenna structures, uses Optical Character Recognition (OCR) to extract dimensional annotations, and uses computer vision techniques like Stable Diffusion Inpainting to isolate clean geometric contours. The reconstructed geometries are turned into vectors and saved as Drawing Exchange Format (DXF) files that can be used in Computer-Aided Design (CAD) programs for electromagnetic simulations. The proposed framework demonstrates the feasibility of transforming unstructured antenna literature into structured, simulation-ready geometric representations by integrating multimodal retrieval, vision-language reasoning, and computer vision techniques. Hence, the design time to verify an antenna design from scientific literature is minimized due to the proposed pipeline.
