TI  - A review of generative autoencoder frameworks and their latent modeling strategies
T2  - APPLIED SOFT COMPUTING
LA  - English
KW  - Adversarial autoencoders
KW  - Variational autoencoders
KW  - Generative autoencoders
KW  - Diffusion-based autoencoders
KW  - Latent space modeling
KW  - SPEECH ENHANCEMENT
KW  - DIFFUSION-MODELS
KW  - NETWORK
KW  - DATASET
AB  - Autoencoder (AE)-based models represent a diverse family of architectures designed for unsupervised learn ing, structured representation compression, controllable data synthesis, etc. Although they are widely applied across image, text, and speech modalities, their latent behavior and generative dynamics remain under-characterized in existing reviews. This survey organizes AE variants according to the way they regulate latent structure, incorporate prior assumptions, and balance reconstruction accuracy with generative diversity. We examine deterministic, probabilistic, adversarial, and diffusion-based AEs through representative architectures, including Sparse, Denoising, and Convolutional Autoencoders; Variational Autoencoder (VAE) derivatives, e.g., beta-VAE, Conditional VAE, Vector Quantized VAE, Wasserstein AE; adversarial frameworks, e.g., Adversarial AE, Introspective VAE, and Adversarial Generator-Encoder Network, VAE-Generative Adversarial Network (VAE-GAN), Bidirectional GAN; and diffusion-based frameworks employing latent autoencoding, e.g., Latent Diffusion Model and Diffusion Autoencoders. For each, we analyze latent constraints, architectural trade-offs, and recurring issues such as posterior collapse, mode imbalance, and codebook degradation. We also discuss hybrid architec tures that combine latent inference with iterative or adversarial generation strategies. Further, case studies in image inpainting, speech denoising, and topic modeling illustrate how latent mechanisms affect learning behavior and application outcomes. Overall, this review consolidates recent advances through a unified latent-space-centered taxonomy and comparative analysis, providing a comprehensive perspective on AE-based generative frameworks across diverse application domains.
