TI  - Who Benefits and Who Is Replaced? Demographic Patterns of Generative AI Exposure in the United States
T2  - STATISTICAL SCIENCE: FROM THEORY TO APPLIED RESEARCH IV, SIS-FENSTATS 2026
LA  - English
CP  - 53rd Italian Statistical Society Meeting-SIS
KW  - AI
KW  - Labour Market
KW  - Demographic Stratification
KW  - USA
AB  - This paper examines the diffusion of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) across socio-demographic groups within the United States (US) working-age population. To this end, we build novel occupation-level indicators of GenAI exposure based on task-based evaluations by Large Language Models. Specifically, occupational task descriptions from O*NET are scored according to the extent to which they can be complemented or replaced by GenAI, and these assessments are then aggregated at the occupational level. By merging exposure indicators with microdata from the 2024 American Community Survey, we examine how the probability of employment in highly exposed occupations varies across socio-demographic groups using logistic regression models. Results reveal substantial heterogeneity by gender, wages and educational attainment. Exposure to GenAI increases with wage and follows an inverted U-shaped pattern across education levels. Men are more likely to be employed in jobs with high complementarity, while women face a higher probability of working in occupations with a higher replacement risk. Additional analyses underscore that fathers of children under age 5 are more likely to be in occupations with low GenAI replacement, while mothers with two or more children are disproportionately represented in such occupations. Overall, these findings reveal non-negligible socio-demographic differences in occupational exposure to GenAI, which may, in turn, reinforce pre-existing labor market inequalities.
