AB  - Peanut roots serve as the primary organ for nutrient absorption and storage, and their spatio-temporal variations in nitrogen (N), phosphorus (P), and potassium (K) contents strongly influence yield formation. However, root samples are difficult to obtain due to their subterranean nature, and the complex nonlinear relationship between hyperspectral signals and nutrient contents limits the accuracy of traditional inversion models. This study proposed an AutoEncoder-Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model-Time-series Large Language Model (AE-DDIMTimeLLM) framework that integrates physics-constrained generative augmentation with large-model semantic regression to generate peanut root hyperspectral data and achieve multi-element joint prediction across growth stages. In the AE stage, physical priors, such as reflectance range, band-group smoothness, and spectral coherence were embedded to ensure physically consistent latent representations. In the DDIM stage, Wavelength Positional Encoding (WPE), Bandwise Attention, and Dual-Domain block were introduced to further improve spectral structural fidelity. As a result, the generated spectra achieved superior performance in PSNR, MRAE, RMSE, and Valley-Corr, surpassing conventional generative models in both structural accuracy and physical realism. Based on the generated data, the TimeLLM model employed feature-driven prompt reconstruction to jointly predict N, P, and K contents across growth stages, yielding a 5-12% increase in R2p and approximately 20% reduction in RMSEp. Moreover, the study identified an optimal augmentation ratio that maximized model generalization without overfitting. These results demonstrate that integrating spectral physical constraints with Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) and semantic spectral-element modeling provides a promising pathway for accurate subsurface nutrient inversion, offering new data-driven insights for precision fertilization and nutrient management in peanut cultivation.
