KW  - ALGORITHM
KW  - DIFFUSION
AB  - Noise reduction (NR) is a critical domain that enhances speech quality and intelligibility in various ap plications. This review presents a comprehensive analysis of noise reduction techniques, traversing from traditional signal processing methods to cutting-edge deep learning approaches. Traditional methods, in cluding Spectral Subtraction, Wiener Filtering, Adaptive Filters (Least Mean Square(LMS), Recursive Least Squares(RLS), Kalman), Wavelet Transform, and Independent Component Analysis, are discussed, highlighting their foundational contributions and inherent limitations, such as musical noise artifacts and dependency on stationary noise characteristics. The advent of deep learning has revolutionized the field, introducing power ful models based on Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), Autoencoders, Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), and Diffusion Models. These data-driven approaches demonstrate supe rior performance in complex and non-stationary noise environments. Furthermore, the review examines hybrid methodologies that combine classical techniques with deep learning to achieve enhanced robustness. The re search also examines the recent transition towards semantic-aware, efficient generative frameworks, particularly Flow-Based Generative Models and Large Language Models (LLMs). These methods exhibit enhanced efficacy in intricate situations by utilizing deterministic probability flows and comprehensive language reasoning, re spectively. Key evaluation metrics, both objective Perceptual Evaluation of Speech Quality(PESQ), Short-Time Objective Intelligibility(STOI), Signal-to-Distortion Ratio(SDR), Mean Squared Error(MSE), and subjective Mean Opinion Score(MOS), as well as widely used datasets, are detailed. Finally, challenges such as computational complexity, generalization to unseen noise, data dependency, and real-time processing are addressed, along with emerging trends like multimodal fusion and model optimization for edge deployment.
