TI  - GPD4TS: A generative pretrained diffusion paradigm for time series forecasting
T2  - PATTERN RECOGNITION
LA  - English
KW  - Time series
KW  - Diffusion models
KW  - Generative paradigm
KW  - Forecasting
AB  - Recently, diffusion models have shown great potential in temporal modeling, such as language modeling, demonstrating the effectiveness of this paradigm for sequential modeling. However, existing generative approaches for time series forecasting often lack forward-looking perspectives. Most methods adhere to conventional settings, such as training and evaluation on small, single-domain datasets or proposing solutions tailored to specific tasks. Such practices limit the generalization and modeling unification of these methods in multi-task and multi-domain scenarios. To address these limitations, we propose a generative pretrained diffusion paradigm for time series forecasting (GPD4TS), a diffusion-based generative model trained from scratch under a pretraining and supervised fine-tuning (SFT) paradigm. GPD4TS models the data distribution through standard diffusion processes and optimizes a variational evidence lower bound (ELBO)-a formal lower bound on the data log-likelihood, unlike the ad hoc reconstruction losses used by some discriminative methods. We further introduce a structure-aware gating SFT strategy, which significantly boosts forecasting performance in downstream, task-specific scenarios. Our evaluation centers on time series forecasting, with brief proof-of-concept demonstrations of the pretrained backbone's zero-shot transferability to imputation and classification. Across a wide range of forecasting benchmarks, we demonstrate that the pre-training paradigm exhibits superior generalization, unification, and zero-shot transfer capabilities compared to prior approaches. Specifically, in cross-domain applications, zero-shot learning, and long-term forecasting tasks, GPD4TS outperforms LLM-based baselines by an average of 15% while using only 1/9.4 of their parameter count. Notably, whereas prior generative time series models consistently fall short of discriminative baselines, GPD4TS is among the first generative approaches to surpass them, even rivaling SOTA methods in performance.
