KW  - ARTIFICIAL-INTELLIGENCE
KW  - MEDICINE
KW  - MACHINE
AB  - The diffusion of generative AI (GenAI) and large language models (LLMs) is reshaping knowledge production, labor organization, institutional decision-making, and public governance. Existing research has identified ethical principles and governance challenges, but often treats functional redistribution, human-AI cooperation, alignment, adaptation, and accountability as separate concerns. This article introduces the 3 & times; 2A Framework as a profile-based interpretive approach for coordinating these concerns in situated AI-mediated institutional configurations. It examines six interdependent axes organized into three diagnostic dyads: automation-augmentation, alliance-alignment, and adaptation-accountability. Rather than reducing AI governance to static compliance or technical optimization, the framework treats human-AI interaction as dynamic, context-sensitive, and institutionally situated. Its primary output is the six-axis 3 & times; 2A profile, which reveals functional, relational, adaptive, and accountability-related tensions. The article clarifies the epistemic status of the framework's outputs. Qualitative profile bands are ordered, evidence-informed interpretive categories rather than numerical or cardinal scores. Numerical scoring, where methodologically justified, requires explicit rubrics, evidence standards, scoring rationales, and interpretive limits. The 3 & times; 2A quotient may therefore be used only as a secondary and conditional synthesis of an established profile, subject to explicit activity-axis judgments, evidential bases, weighting assumptions, aggregation rules, and interpretive limitations. A worked benchmark re-analysis of the publicly documented Province of Frysl & acirc;n environmental-monitoring case illustrates procedural use without claiming empirical validation. The case is represented through an ordinal qualitative six-axis profile, without numerical scoring or a composite quotient. The framework thus offers a profile-first, evidence-sensitive, and epistemically modest approach to reflective and accountable human-AI governance.
