AB  - Zero Waste, the principle of minimizing waste through redesigned production and consumption patterns, has grown into a globally visible lifestyle movement. Whether and how social media platforms shape the diffusion of this movement across communities and geographies remains, however, an open empirical question. This study investigates the structure and dynamics of Zero Waste discourse on Twitter (now X) between May 2007 and March 2023, drawing on a dataset of 686,666 tweets from 197,633 unique users worldwide. Using the Social Media Analysis based on Hashtag Research framework, we apply hashtag frequency analysis, topic modeling, geographic distribution analysis, and sentiment analysis to map the thematic landscape of online Zero Waste communication and to identify how geographical context shapes community engagement and discourse. The analysis reveals nine dominant discussion clusters, ranging from food waste and plastic reduction to circular economy and fashion waste, and shows that although the United States generates the highest absolute tweet volume, Germany leads in per-capita engagement. Sentiment is predominantly positive, with the notable exception of plastic waste discourse. Beyond describing online behavior, this study demonstrates that large-scale social media analysis can serve as an early-warning instrument for detecting shifts in values, coalition formation, and discursive convergence that may precede institutional and policy change. We thus contribute to sustainability science and to the Ecology and Society research community by proposing digital discourse as a legitimate analytical unit within the study of social-ecological transitions, and by providing empirical evidence that online sustainability narratives are geographically uneven, an insight with direct implications for the design of targeted communication strategies by NGOs, policy makers, and sustainability practitioners.
