TI  - Refining skeleton with text: A diffusion-enhanced contrastive learning framework for action recognition
T2  - NEUROCOMPUTING
LA  - English
KW  - Skeleton-based action recognition
KW  - Multi-modality
KW  - Contrastive learning
AB  - With the rapid development of multimodal learning, the recent research on skeleton-based action recognition combined with text features has received significant attention. However, existing methods have limited repre sentation of temporal relationships in text cues, failing to capture the temporal dynamic changes of actions. Furthermore, existing methods have not effectively aligned skeleton and text modalities; they only aligned intrasequence information (i.e., aligning skeleton and text features within a single action sequence), which overlooked inter-sequence and inter-class feature alignment. To address these issues, a novel diffusion-enhanced contrastive learning framework (hereafter referred to as ReST-Diff) has been proposed in this paper, which leverages tem porally enhanced text cues to refine skeleton features while achieving cross-modal alignment at a global level. Specifically, a pre-trained Large Language Model (LLM) has been employed as a knowledge engine to automat ically generate multi-level text descriptions, which cover body parts, time intervals, and global semantics of actions. In the meantime, a diffusion alignment module has been presented to reshape the alignment task into a conditional denoising process, thereby learning the correspondence between text descriptions and spatiotempo ral skeleton features. Finally, a global contrastive learning module with a memory mechanism has been utilized to construct a globally discriminative feature space, which significantly enhances the model's ability to distin guish similar actions. Experimental results on 3 benchmark datasets demonstrate that ReST-Diff has achieved satisfactory results compared to other state-of-the-art methods.
