TI  - Pressure reconstruction for airfoil gust response based on sparse measurement points
T2  - PHYSICS OF FLUIDS
LA  - English
KW  - SIMULATION
KW  - AEROFOIL
KW  - MODEL
AB  - To address the complex unsteady flow phenomena encountered by aircraft in gust, traditional gust sensing methods, such as wingtip acceleration and root bending moment measurements, are limited by installation space, resulting in the acquisition of only localized and discrete flow information. It is impossible to sense the complete information of the flow field around the aircraft. Thus, the real-time gust pressure reconstruction models are developed in this study using compressed sensing based on proper orthogonal decomposition (CS-POD) and Gappy POD (G-POD), respectively. First, proper orthogonal decomposition (POD) modes are extracted based on computational fluid dynamics simulations under multi-frequency sinusoidal gust conditions, and reconstruction of the airfoil surface pressure under various gust scenarios is achieved through the solution of the temporal coefficients. The effects of the number and distribution of measurement points, as well as noise, on reconstruction accuracy, generalization capability, and robustness were systematically analyzed. Finally, wind tunnel tests were carried out to validate the engineering applicability of the proposed model. In contrast to G-POD, which requires five measurement points, comparable accuracy and robustness are achieved by the CS-POD with only three measurement points. In addition, wind tunnel tests validate the applicability of the CS-POD in real engineering environments. Despite the presence of factors such as model vibration and three-dimensional effects, the real-time gust pressure reconstruction model constructed using the CS-POD can still accurately reconstruct the surface pressure distribution of the airfoil.
