KW  - TRANSFORMER
AB  - Lyric-to-song generation aims to create complete musical pieces combining expressive vocals and instrumental accompaniment directly from textual lyrics. While recent advances in autoregressive (AR) language models and non-autoregressive (NAR) diffusion models have significantly improved music and short-form song generation, producing long-form, high-fidelity, and structurally coherent songs remains a substantial challenge. To address this issue, we present SongBloom, a unified AR-diffusion framework for long-form lyric-to-song generation. Conditioned on structured lyrics and a short reference audio clip, SongBloom interleaves semantic sketch prediction with acoustic latent synthesis, enabling coarse-grained structural planning and fine-grained timbral modeling to mutually inform each other. Semantic steps produce high-level discrete tokens that act as pseudo chain-of-thought guidance, while acoustic steps generate continuous latent patches via a diffusion module. This bidirectional interaction ensures both global coherence and high audio fidelity. To further enhance performance, we design a three-stage training pipeline comprising large-scale pretraining, supervised finetuning, and direct preference optimization (DPO)-based post-training. This pipeline progressively improves the model's perceptual alignment and robustness. Extensive objective metrics and human listening evaluations demonstrate that SongBloom surpasses all non-commercial baselines and approaches or exceeds the performance of leading commercial systems, delivering expressive, high-quality, and structurally coherent full-length songs up to 150 seconds. The full model weights and inference code are released to facilitate future research.
