KW  - IDENTIFICATION
AB  - Accurately recovering missing structural responses is essential in structural health monitoring (SHM) for ensuring data integrity and structural safety. Current recovery models suffer from two limitations: training only on undamaged structure-state vibration data leads to poor practicality and generalizability when structural damage occurs; pure time-domain modeling lacks representation and integration of structural prior information, making it difficult to capture damage-induced vibration pattern changes. This study proposes a multi-modal diffusion model (M-DM) for missing response recovery under various damage scenarios. It leverages the capability of generative artificial intelligence to recover missing signals from available signals. A bridge vibration dataset covering a wide range of damage cases is first established via finite element parametric modeling, which serves as the basis for model training and evaluation. To enrich raw time-domain features utilizing structural prior information, a large language model and Continuous Wavelet Transform are adopted to extract dense representations from damage inspection texts and monitoring data. Cross-attention is then introduced to efficiently align and fuse these multi-modal heterogeneous features, guiding the diffusion model's generation process to enhance the quality of recovered signals. Ablation experiment results show M-DM outperforms singlemodality models in time and frequency domains. Its multi-modal fusion enhances the accuracy of response recovery and modal analysis across damage cases by injecting structural prior information into the model. The proposed M-DM provides a feasible solution for missing response recovery considering structural damages, and its flexible framework can efficiently adapt to newly introduced across-modal representations to better support related SHM tasks.
