AB  - In mission-critical applications of unmanned and autonomous aerial systems, it is of significant importance to develop robust fault-tolerant strategies that can countermeasure system degradation and consequently support the integration into the National Air Space. This paper presents preliminary results that illustrate comparison between two types of discrete adaptive control algorithms, direct and indirect, and their application to fault-tolerance capabilities. A set of performance metrics are defined to perform the analysis in terms of control effort, tracking performance and reconfiguration of control laws under commonly occurring failures such as actuator delay, uncertain ice accretion on surfaces of the aircraft, and partial surface control damage. Both control architectures are applied to a discrete state-space system derived from an auto-regressive moving-average model to evaluate control accuracy and the ability to adapt to disturbances.
