AB  - This paper presents the results of the 1(st) AIAA Stability and Control Prediction Workshop obtained at the U.S. Air Force Academy using kCFD flow solver of HPCMP CREATE (TM)-AV Kestrel simulation tools. Test case considered is 1/16.835 of the so-called Common Research Model (CRM). The geometry is the same as that used in the AIAA 6(th) Drag Prediction Workshop with addition of a horizontal tail and a vertical tail designed by ONERA. The geometry, however, does not include engine nacelles and pylons. Test case was tested at the ONERA S1MA wind tunnel at different Mach and Reynolds numbers for fixed lift coefficient values at zero and non-zero sideslip angles. The computational results include a grid sensitivity analysis and validation with available experimental data. In addition, the Mach number effects on the predicted stability and control values of the test case are shown and compared with experimental data. Finally, a system identification method is developed and used for prediction of stability and control data. The method uses a varying-frequency change in the angle of attack and side-slip angle as an input signal (or training maneuver). Once the model is created and the unknowns were estimated from the input signal, it was used to predict the static and dynamic data of the test case. The results show that the model predictions match well with calculated CFD data at discrete flow conditions.
