TY  - JOUR
AU  - Tian, HH
AU  - Xie, XL
TI  - Principal-direction-frame diagnostics of near-wall vorticity transport over a stationary and deforming Gaussian bump in duct flow
T2  - PHYSICS OF FLUIDS
LA  - English
KW  - SUPPRESSION
AB  - Boundary vorticity flux (BVF) and boundary enstrophy flux (BEF) characterize the transport of vorticity and enstrophy at solid walls. For curved and deformable boundaries, interpreting these fluxes in relation to local surface geometry requires a direction-resolved representation that is independent of surface parametrization. Existing formulations in the principal-direction non-holonomic frame provide such a theoretical description, but their application to numerical flow fields requires consistent transformations of both physical components and the spatial derivatives involved in BEF and BVF. Building on this theoretical basis, the present study develops a computational procedure that connects numerical flow fields represented in general curvilinear coordinates with principal-direction analyses. Explicit transformation relations are derived for the required velocity and vorticity components and their first- and second-order derivatives, with the spatial variations of the transformation coefficients and local basis vectors retained. The geometrical consistency of the transformed quantities is assessed through the Gauss-Codazzi relations, and the resulting formulation enables term-by-term evaluation of the BEF and BVF decompositions from discrete flow data. The procedure is demonstrated for laminar flow at Re=200 through a square duct with a stationary or periodically deforming Gaussian bump. In the stationary-wall case, the decomposition distinguishes the dominant effect of near-wall shear gradients from the local curvature-weighted modulation of the BEF. Sign changes in the curvature-weighted contribution occur near surface-type boundaries identified by K-G=0, illustrating the coupled influence of surface curvature and near-wall vorticity. The BVF analysis further identifies strong competing contributions whose cancelation is obscured in the total flux. At the deformation extrema, the results distinguish the effects associated with instantaneous surface geometry from the unsteady near-wall response retained from the preceding wall motion. The developed procedure thereby makes geometry-resolved BEF and BVF analyses accessible to numerical investigations of curved and deformable wall flows under different configurations and flow conditions.
