TI  - Digital Cosmos: The Evolution of Academic Knowledge in Turkey, Epistemic Isomorphism, and the Reconstruction of Meaning
T2  - SIRNAK UNIVERSITY JOURNAL OF DIVINITY FACULTY
LA  - English
KW  - Sociology of religion
KW  - the evolution of academic knowledge
KW  - digital cosmos
KW  - epistemic isomorphism
KW  - BERTopic
KW  - SHAP ve t-SNE machine learning methods
KW  - INDICATORS
AB  - This study investigates the thematic evolution and epistemological dynamics of academic knowledge production in Turkey through a deep learning-based analysis of 3,749 master's and doctoral theses published between 1978 and 2024. Drawing on advanced machine learning methodologies-particularly BERTopic, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations), and t-SNE (t distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding)-the purpose of this research is to identify thematic clusters, semantic structures, and temporal transformations across multiple disciplines and institutions. By analysing master's and doctoral theses over time (1978-2024), the research question of this paper is how do institutional, linguistic, and socio-technical factors shape the thematic evolution and epistemological dynamics of academic knowledge production in Turkiye?" To answer such questions, it pursues a computational mixed-methods approach, combining deep learning-based topic modeling, explainable AI (XAI), and network analysis. Based on an extensive research, the study finds that the linguistic variable, particularly the language of thesis composition, plays the most significant role in shaping thematic distributions, as evidenced by SHAP value analysis (|SHAP| > 11.58). Institutional and disciplinary classifications also exert notable influence, though to a lesser extent, suggesting a phenomenon of epistemic isomorphism in academic settings. High-dimensional semantic analysis reveals that the thematic area of digitalization forms a heterogeneous and expansive conceptual space. In contrast, domains such as machine learning and blockchain technologies exhibit high semantic cohesion and consolidation, indicating the emergence of semantically polarized paradigms. Semantic network analyses identify the digitalization theme as the central node, functioning as "semantic mediator" that bridges diverse epistemic communities, an insight aligned with Latour's actor-network theory and the Mode 2 knowledge production model by Gibbons et al. The study concludes that academic knowledge production is a dynamic, complex, and adaptive process shaped by linguistic, institutional, and socio-technical factors. Its findings not only enhance our understanding of disciplinary convergence and knowledge diffusion but also suggest strategic directions for fostering interdisciplinary research and policy planning.
